        "title": "Historical Traditions - Egyptian, Mughal, Byzantine, Art Nouveau/Deco",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "75,000 BCE  Blombos Cave shell beads (earliest adornment — South Africa)\n~3000 BCE   Egyptian Old Kingdom goldsmithing — foundational techniques\n~1350 BCE   Tutankhamun's tomb — finest preserved ancient collection\n~600 BCE    Etruscan granulation mastery — peak of ancient technique\n~400 BCE    Hellenistic period — color stones added to Greek tradition\n~100 CE     Roman cameos, intaglios, empire-wide trade in gems\n~400 CE     Byzantine Empire — cloisonné enamel on gold\n~700–1400   Islamic tradition — Moorish, Fatimid, Ottoman\n~1200       Medieval European — guild system, reliquaries\n~1526       Mughal Empire — jadau setting, kundan, meenakari\n~1700       Georgian era — closed-back settings, foil, paste\n~1837       Victorian — mourning jewelry, archaeological revival\n~1890       Art Nouveau — Lalique, plique-à-jour, nature motifs\n~1920       Art Deco — geometric, platinum, calibré stones\n  2000+     Contemporary — CAD, lab-grown, sustainable sourcing\n```"
